msgid "Extensions and trust"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*What a package, a plugin, a repository and a signature are, and what a "
"signature guarantees.*"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"EMStudio can be extended by other people: a project adds the properties "
"of its kind of study, a laboratory its recording sheet, a programmer a "
"command that reads a format. Each extension asks for some trust, and the "
"amount depends on what it carries. EMStudio says plainly what each piece "
"guarantees, so that the part left to your judgement is clear."
msgstr ""

msgid "Data and code"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"An extension carries data, code, or both, and the difference is the one "
"that matters most."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A **community package** carries **data only**: vocabularies, properties "
"for a kind of study, extractors and sets of canonical points to place on "
"a model, recording sheets, colour schemes. Every tool of the Extended "
"Matrix reads it, and it runs nothing. The worst a package can do is "
"describe something badly."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A **plugin** carries **Python code**: commands, importers and exporters, "
"checks, panels. On your computer a Python plugin has the powers of your "
"user, as any program you run does."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A **mixed package** carries both. You can install its data alone, with "
"**Data only**, and add the code later."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Extensions are installed on the computer, not in a study, and EM Tools in"
" Blender uses the same ones. A study declares the community packages it "
"uses, with their version, as it declares its vocabularies (see "
":doc:`vocabularies`)."
msgstr ""

msgid "The trust question"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Before installing a plugin, EMStudio shows its panel **Code**: the "
"permissions it asks and the reason it gives for each, what it can do "
"(*read the graph*, *write the graph*), and where its commands appear. The"
" answer you give, **Install everything**, **Data only** or **Cancel**, is"
" your trust in that code."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The question comes back only when it has something new to ask: when a new"
" version asks for more, can do more, or comes from elsewhere. An update "
"that changes none of these installs with the trust already given. A "
"plugin loaded from a development folder, for those who write plugins, is "
"marked **development, not verified**."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Every act of a plugin is recorded in the study with the plugin that made "
"it, so that a value written by a plugin can always be told apart from one"
" written by a person."
msgstr ""

msgid "Repositories"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A **repository** is a list of extensions published in one place: the "
"official list of the Extended Matrix, the list of a project on the "
"internet, or a folder on the laboratory's network. Its index gives, for "
"every archive, a fingerprint computed from the archive's bytes. EMStudio "
"checks every archive against that fingerprint before opening it, so what "
"you install is exactly what the index names."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Reading a repository on the internet needs **Allow online access**, a "
"single switch for the whole computer; a repository in a folder works "
"without it."
msgstr ""

msgid "What a signature guarantees"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A repository can be **signed**. Whoever runs it holds a secret key and "
"publishes the matching public key; you give EMStudio that public key when"
" you add the repository. From then on, its index and its archives are "
"taken only when they carry a valid signature of that key."
msgstr ""

msgid "A signature guarantees two things:"
msgstr ""

msgid "**who published the bytes**: the holder of the key you chose to trust;"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**that the bytes arrived unchanged**: an archive altered on the way, or "
"on the server, no longer matches its signature and is refused."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A signature speaks of origin and integrity: these are the bytes the "
"repository published. The quality and the intentions of the code are a "
"separate question, and whether the repository deserves your trust is a "
"judgement about the people behind it."
msgstr ""

msgid "Reviews"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"What a signature leaves open, a **review** can answer in part. A "
"repository can list the plugins whose code a person has read, with who "
"read it and when. If you tick **Trust the reviews of this repository**, a"
" reviewed plugin installs without the trust question and shows **reviewed"
" by … on …**. A review is the judgement of a person, and its worth is the"
" worth of that person's reading; you decide, repository by repository, "
"whose reviews to accept."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"EMStudio shows the two marks apart: **signed by the key … of …** for "
"where the bytes come from, **reviewed by … on …** for who read the code."
msgstr ""

msgid "Plugins of the node"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A StratiGraph node can run plugins for a room, and there the question of "
"trust is answered differently, because the code runs on a shared server. "
"Only the node's administrator installs them, and each runs in a closed "
"space: no network, none of the node's files, a time limit and limits on "
"what it may use. The node first proves that the closed space holds; when "
"it cannot prove it, its plugins stay off. The owner of a room turns a "
"plugin on for that room, and what the plugin writes arrives as changes of"
" the room, signed by the person who ran it and by the plugin."
msgstr ""

msgid "What stays with you"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Fingerprints, signatures and the closed space of the node are checked by "
"the program. Three things remain matters of trust, and EMStudio leaves "
"them to you:"
msgstr ""

msgid "the people who hold the key of a repository;"
msgstr ""

msgid "the people whose reviews you accept;"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"a plugin installed from disk with no signature and no review, whose code "
"you accept by answering the trust question."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The how-to pages describe the gestures: :doc:`/how-to/extensions/install-"
"packages-and-plugins` and :doc:`/how-to/extensions/repositories-"
"signatures-updates`."
msgstr ""

